rCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 37","reason":"If each column sums to T, then S=(100+10+1)T=111T=3·37T. The one-term sequence 111 is allowed, so no prime other than 3 or 37 always divides S.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_p055"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_12A_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p153","cnm_p157"],"id":"equal-column-sums","label":"Compare the digit-column sums","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-column-sums":["cnm_p055","cnm_011","math:prime-factorization"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p055","cnm_011","math:prime-factorization","cnm_p153","cnm_p157"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:064b2f2b4b61b0a5e06fb6d4"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 4","reason":"Since n<2007, S(n)≤28 and S(S(n))≤10, so 1969≤n≤2006. Modulo 9 the equation gives 3n≡0, hence 3 divides n. Checking those candidates yields exactly 1977,1980,1983,2001.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_p055"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_12A_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p053","cnm_029"],"id":"bound-and-split-decimal-blocks","label":"Bound n and examine its final digits","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bound-and-split-decimal-blocks":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_p055","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_p055","cnm_042","cnm_p053","cnm_029"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:064b2f2b4b61b0a5e06fb6d4"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (16)","reason":"Let the lower square be n². Subtracting the two numbers gives 9(c-b)=(2n+1)/3, so 2n+1=27(c-b). Three-digit bounds give 10≤n≤31; the only odd multiple of 27 in the resulting gap range is 27. Thus n=13, abc=169+9=178, and a+b+c=16.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_p055"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_12B_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p055","cnm_p153","cnm_p156","cnm_148"],"id":"square-gap-and-digit-difference","label":"Compare the two equal steps","methodDirectNodeIds":{"square-gap-and-digit-difference":["math:integer-diophantine-equations"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_p055","cnm_p153","cnm_p156","cnm_148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:064b2f2b4b61b0a5e06fb6d4"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 1/5","reason":"A palindrome abba equals 1001a+110b, where 1<=a<=9 and 0<=b<=9. Modulo 7 this is 5b, so b must be 0 or 7. There are 18 favorable palindromes among 90, giving 1/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_p055"],"coverage":"partial","flags":["Chosen at random is interpreted as uniformly chosen from the 90 four-digit palindromes."],"questionId":"2010_AMC_10B_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p055","cnm_h231","cnm_p153"],"id":"palindrome-place-value","label":"Use the palindrome's digit structure","methodDirectNodeIds":{"palindrome-place-value":["cnm_285","math:modular-congruence"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_285","math:modular-congruence","cnm_p055","cnm_h231","cnm_p153"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:064b2f2b4b61b0a5e06fb6d4"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 37","reason":"If H is the sum of the hundreds digits, the tens and units digits also sum to H. Hence S = 100H+10H+H = 111H = 3·37H. The valid cycle (123, 231, 312) sums to 666 = 2·3^2·37, which has no prime factor larger than 37.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_p055"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_10A_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p055","cnm_p114","cnm_p153","cnm_p157"],"id":"compare-digit-column-sums","label":"Compare the three digit-column sums","methodDirectNodeIds":{"compare-digit-column-sums":["cnm_155","math:prime-factorization"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_155","math:prime-factorization","cnm_p055","cnm_p114","cnm_p153","cnm_p157"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:064b2f2b4b61b0a5e06fb6d4"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E (1/5)","reason":"A four-digit palindrome has value 1001a+110b, with 1≤a≤9 and 0≤b≤9, giving 90 possibilities. Modulo 7 its value is 5b, so divisibility requires b = 0 or 7. There are 9×2 = 18 favorable palindromes, yielding 18/90 = 1/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_p055"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_12B_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p055","cnm_p153","cnm_p165"],"id":"digit-divisibility-count","label":"Count by the two free digits","methodDirectNodeIds":{"digit-divisibility-count":["cnm_285","cnm_h231","math:modular-congruence"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_285","cnm_h231","math:modular-congruence","cnm_p055","cnm_p153","cnm_p165"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:064b2f2b4b61b0a5e06fb6d4"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 3/5","reason":"There are 4×5×5×5=500 integers. With an even tens digit, divisibility by 4 requires the units digit to be 0, 4, or 8. Thus 4×5×5×3=300 qualify, giving 300/500=3/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_p055"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2026_AMC_8_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p055","cnm_p156","cnm_p165","cnm_p168"],"id":"digit-choices","label":"Count the allowed digit choices","methodDirectNodeIds":{"digit-choices":["cnm_p153","cnm_h231"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p153","cnm_h231","cnm_p055","cnm_p156","cnm_p165","cnm_p168"]}]}],"canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_p055"],"label":"2.NBT.A.1b","layer":"US","meaning":"Practice covers aligned canonical concepts, with no claim of complete standard coverage or mastery.","nextOffset":null,"sourceNode":{"code":"2.NBT.A.1b","description":"The numbers 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900 refer to one, two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight, or nine hundreds (and 0 tens and 0 ones).","id":"73","label":"2.NBT.A.1b","metadata":{"clusterId":"18","clusterName":"Understand Place Value.","clusterOrdinal":"A","domainCode":"NBT","domainId":"8","domainName":"Number And Operations In Base Ten","ordinal":"1.b","sourceCode":"2.NBT.A.1.b"},"parentSourceId":"71","placement":{"grade":"2","meaning":"Native Common Core grade"},"reviewStatus":"source_only"},"sourceNodeId":"73","status":"partial","total":60}
